      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/252183",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/9789241511766-eng.dc.json",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "who-iris/library/9789241511766-eng"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/9789241549912-eng",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "WHO recommendations on antenatal care for a positive pregnancy experience",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2016-11-07",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789241549912",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/250796",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This guideline presents the World Health Organization’s evidence-based recommendations on routine antenatal care to support a positive pregnancy experience for pregnant women and adolescent girls. It aims to provide a comprehensive, consolidated framework for antenatal care that complements existing WHO guidance on the management of specific pregnancy-related complications. The recommendations promote a person-centred, rights-based approach that extends beyond the prevention of maternal and perinatal morbidity and mortality to enhance women’s physical, psychological and sociocultural well-being, support healthy pregnancies, and improve the quality and utilization of antenatal care. Intended for policy-makers, programme managers and health professionals, the guideline informs the development of national policies, clinical protocols and service delivery models.\n\nDeveloped according to the WHO guideline development process, the document draws on systematic reviews and applies the GRADE and GRADE-CERQual approaches together with the DECIDE evidence-to-decision framework. It contains 49 recommendations covering nutritional interventions, maternal and fetal assessment, preventive measures, management of common physiological symptoms, and health system interventions to improve antenatal care utilization and quality. The guideline also introduces the 2016 WHO antenatal care model, recommending a minimum of eight contacts during pregnancy, and emphasizes implementation, dissemination and periodic updating as new evidence becomes available.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/9789241549912-eng.dc.json",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "who-iris/library/9789241549912-eng"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/9789241513111-eng",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "National health workforce accounts: a handbook",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2017-10-26",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789241513111",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/259360",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This handbook introduces the National Health Workforce Accounts (NHWA), a standardized framework developed by the World Health Organization to strengthen health workforce information systems and support evidence-based workforce planning and policy-making. The publication is intended to assist countries in monitoring and analysing health workforce capacity, distribution, education, labour market dynamics, financing, governance and information systems in the context of universal health coverage and the health-related Sustainable Development Goals. It outlines the rationale for improved health workforce data and highlights the role of harmonized metrics in addressing challenges related to workforce availability, accessibility, performance and retention.\n\nThe handbook presents a modular framework comprising 78 core indicators organized across 10 modules covering active workforce stock, education and training, regulation and accreditation, education financing, labour market flows, employment conditions, workforce spending and remuneration, skill mix, governance, and health workforce information systems. For each indicator, it provides definitions, methodological guidance, reporting requirements, data sources and disaggregation options. The publication also describes implementation principles, reporting standards and potential applications of the NHWA, supporting countries in strengthening health workforce monitoring, facilitating international comparability and informing policies aimed at improving health system performance and population access to quality health services.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/9789241513111-eng.dc.json",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "who-iris/library/9789241513111-eng"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/who-his-hwf-nhwa-20171-eng",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Report"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Understanding national health workforce accounts",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2017-12-31",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/275758",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "Understanding National Health Workforce Accounts (NHWA) is an introductory guidance document developed by the World Health Organization to explain the purpose, policy relevance and structure of the National Health Workforce Accounts framework. The publication outlines how NHWA supports the standardization of health workforce information systems, improves the quality and comparability of data, and strengthens the evidence base for health workforce policy, planning and monitoring. Positioned within the context of universal health coverage, the Sustainable Development Goals, the Global Strategy on Human Resources for Health: Workforce 2030, and related international commitments, the document highlights the importance of reliable workforce information for addressing current and future health system challenges.\n\nThe publication describes the benefits of NHWA at national, regional and global levels and presents key policy questions that can be informed through systematic workforce data collection and analysis. It introduces the health labour market framework used by NHWA, emphasizing workforce production, labour market flows, distribution, productivity and regulation. The document also summarizes the development process of the NHWA indicators and explains the modular structure of the framework, which comprises ten modules covering workforce stock, education, labour market dynamics, employment conditions, financing, governance, planning and information systems. Together, these elements provide a foundation for evidence-informed decision-making and strengthened health workforce monitoring.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/who-his-hwf-nhwa-20171-eng.dc.json",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "who-iris/library/who-his-hwf-nhwa-20171-eng"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/who-rhr-1806-eng",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Report"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Classification of digital health interventions v1.0: a shared language to describe the uses of digital technology for health",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2018-03-14",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/260480",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "This publication presents version 1.0 of the World Health Organization’s Classification of Digital Health Interventions (DHIs), a taxonomy developed to provide a shared and standardized language for describing how digital and mobile technologies are used to address health system needs. Intended primarily for public health audiences, the framework supports communication among policymakers, programme planners, implementers, researchers, donors and technology specialists by defining digital health interventions as discrete digital functionalities linked to specific health objectives. The classification was developed to facilitate evidence synthesis, landscape analyses, planning processes and the articulation of digital solutions to identified health system challenges.\n\nThe framework organizes digital health interventions into four primary groups according to the main user: clients, health-care providers, health system managers and data services. It links digital interventions to broader health system challenges and to system categories such as electronic medical records, logistics management information systems and telemedicine platforms. Developed through technical consultations, literature review and public feedback, the classification builds on existing mHealth and eHealth frameworks while providing a simplified and practical reference for health programmes. The document is intended to support the design, implementation, evaluation and interoperability of digital health initiatives and will be periodically updated to reflect emerging technologies and evolving practices in digital health.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/who-rhr-1806-eng.dc.json",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "who-iris/library/who-rhr-1806-eng"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/9789241514446-eng",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "National health workforce accounts: implementation guide",
      "dcterms:creator": [
        "World Health Organization"
      ],
      "dcterms:issued": "2018-10-19",
      "dcterms:publisher": "World Health Organization",
      "bibo:isbn": "9789241514446",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/275473",
      "dcterms:language": "en",
      "dcterms:abstract": "The National Health Workforce Accounts (NHWA) Implementation Guide provides practical guidance for countries on establishing and sustaining systems to collect, analyse, use and report standardized health workforce data. Developed as a companion document to the NHWA Handbook and in support of the Global Strategy on Human Resources for Health: Workforce 2030, the guide outlines a structured approach to strengthening health workforce information systems and improving the availability, quality and use of human resources for health data. It is intended for policy-makers, programme managers, technical experts and stakeholders involved in health workforce planning, monitoring and governance, and emphasizes collaboration across sectors such as health, education, labour, finance and statistics.\n\nThe guide presents an implementation framework organized into three phases—conceptualization, operationalization, and scaling up and sustainability—comprising eight steps that address political commitment, governance arrangements, planning, legal considerations, data compilation and analysis, validation, dissemination, and process review. It describes the roles of national institutions and multistakeholder coordination mechanisms, provides guidance on data quality, reporting and communication processes, and promotes the integration of existing information systems. By supporting the systematic monitoring of health workforce indicators, the guide aims to strengthen evidence-informed decision-making and contribute to health workforce development, universal health coverage and broader national and global health objectives.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/catalogue/records/9789241514446-eng.dc.json",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "who-iris/library/9789241514446-eng"
    },
    {
      "@id": "references/9789241515610-eng",
      "@type": [
        "fabio:Work",
        "bibo:Book"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Protecting all against tetanus: guide to sustaining maternal and neonatal tetanus elimination (MNTE) and broadening tetanus protection for all populations",
